WEBVTT

00:00:04.500 --> 00:00:08.840
Two agents, one customer, both remember the conversation.

00:00:08.840 --> 00:00:11.140
Neither one knows what the other's about to do.

00:00:13.640 --> 00:00:19.640
Here's Jordan. Jordan got a promise last week I'll email the revised quote after Tuesday.

00:00:19.640 --> 00:00:22.640
Now two agents both pick up the follow-up task at the same instant.

00:00:22.640 --> 00:00:26.640
Agent A and Agent B each query CockroachDB for the same thing.

00:00:26.640 --> 00:00:30.640
Jordan's consent, the open promise, and anything similar in vector memory.

00:00:30.640 --> 00:00:35.640
Both get the same answer back with citations. Both are about to act.

00:00:35.640 --> 00:00:37.640
That's the moment most systems get this wrong.

00:00:39.300 --> 00:00:44.460
Watch the lease. Agent B gets there first. One serializable transaction, one

00:00:44.460 --> 00:00:49.220
fenced lease, one outbox row, committed. Agent A retries a beat later, sees a

00:00:49.220 --> 00:00:53.820
newer fencing token and backs off cleanly. No duplicate email, no coin flip.

00:00:53.820 --> 00:00:58.540
Cockroach DB didn't just store the memory, it decided transactionally who gets to act on it.

00:01:00.080 --> 00:01:05.740
Now Jordan revokes email consent. The email from a moment ago is still sitting in the queue waiting to send.

00:01:06.140 --> 00:01:12.660
Doesn't matter that it was already approved. Before delivery, the worker rechecks consent one more time and this time it's revoked.

00:01:12.660 --> 00:01:15.780
The send gets canceled, not sent before anything goes out.

00:01:17.740 --> 00:01:24.540
One more test. Kill the outbox worker mid delivery. On restart it doesn't guess. It

00:01:24.540 --> 00:01:28.820
rechecks consent, rechecks the fencing token and finds the one pending row it

00:01:28.820 --> 00:01:34.380
already approved. It resumes that and only that. No second send, no lost task.

00:01:35.880 --> 00:01:41.160
Under the hood, CockroachDB Cloud's distributed vector index runs for recall.

00:01:41.160 --> 00:01:46.480
The official ccloud CLI provisioned the database and SQL identity, and AWS Lambda

00:01:46.480 --> 00:01:50.440
serves this live judge console. Bedrock remains fixture-backed because this AWS

00:01:50.440 --> 00:01:54.960
organization explicitly denies Bedrock access. We then ran 1,000 concurrent

00:01:54.960 --> 00:01:58.600
retried attempts against the Cloud database. One approved action, zero

00:01:58.600 --> 00:02:00.920
duplicates, zero consent violations.

00:02:02.420 --> 00:02:06.620
This demo sends through a sandbox provider, not a live inbox.

00:02:06.620 --> 00:02:11.620
And ContactSafe enforces the policy you configure, it isn't a compliance review on its own.

00:02:13.120 --> 00:02:16.440
Shared memory made two agents safe to run together.

00:02:16.440 --> 00:02:18.000
That's ContactSafe.
